# infra-utm-anon-egress secrets
#
# Copy to .env and fill in, or just run `make preflight` -- it creates .env from
# this file with strong generated passwords and mode 0600. .env is gitignored.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env
#
# This is the same split your Terraform repos use (terraform.tfvars vs *.tf):
# lab.conf holds build configuration you could share to reproduce a fleet, while
# .env holds only what must never leave this machine. Everything here is
# OPTIONAL -- an unset value means "do not configure that feature", never a
# broken build.
#
# Secrets reach Ansible through a 0600 temp vars file, never through
# `-e key=value` on the command line, because command-line arguments are visible
# to every user on the host in `ps auxww`.

# --- Console passwords ------------------------------------------------------
# Console/GUI login only. SSH stays key-only on both VMs (ssh_pwauth: false), so
# these never weaken remote access.
#
# Why you want them: cloud-init locks the user's password, which leaves the
# console able to show boot output but not accept a login. For the WORKSTATION
# that is the difference between having and not having a recovery path -- it has
# no host port forward by design, so if the gateway or its own sshd fails, the
# UTM console is the only way in. A Phase 3 desktop greeter also needs one.
#
# Separate passwords per VM on purpose: compartmentalisation is the point of this
# repo, and the gateway is the box that will hold the VPN credentials.
GATEWAY_CONSOLE_PASSWORD=""
WORKSTATION_CONSOLE_PASSWORD=""

# --- Mullvad account ---------------------------------------------------------
# Acquire the account anonymously: Monero or cash. NOT iDEAL or any bank-linked
# method -- that ties the account to your identity and defeats the purpose.
#
# The assembled (or hand-supplied) config below is installed on the GATEWAY
# ONLY and never reaches the workstation.

# --- Exit selection ---------------------------------------------------------
# Pick the exit by name and let the build assemble the multihop config, OR set
# MULLVAD_WG_CONFIG below to one file you assembled yourself. Setting both is an
# error: a silently ignored key is exactly how you end up on an exit you did not
# choose. Leaving BOTH empty is fine too -- the gateway just has no tunnel yet.
#
# MULLVAD_EXIT is the country the internet sees. Free precision:
#   "bg"             any Bulgarian server you have a config for
#   "bg-sof"         Sofia only
#   "bg-sof-wg-001"  exactly that server
# One config per server must exist in secrets/ as <hostname>.conf -- download
# them from Mullvad's config generator (all countries at once is fine).
#
# Empty by default: everything in this file is opt-in, and setting this makes
# `make preflight` fetch Mullvad's relay list over your real IP on the spot.
# Uncomment and edit once you have configs in secrets/:
# MULLVAD_EXIT="bg"
MULLVAD_EXIT=""

# MULLVAD_ENTRY is the server the gateway actually connects to. Same notation.
# Empty means any active server in a country OTHER than the exit's, which is the
# point of the second hop: the entry sees your real IP but not your destination,
# the exit sees your destination but not your IP. The build REFUSES to run if
# entry and exit share a country, or if the pair resolves to the single-hop
# port 51820.
MULLVAD_ENTRY=""

# The chosen pair is pinned in generated/mullvad-exit.chosen so `make configure`
# cannot move your tunnel to another country mid-lab. `make destroy` clears it,
# so every fresh lab gets a fresh pair. Edit MULLVAD_EXIT and the pin is
# discarded on the next run.

# Escape hatch: one hand-assembled config, used verbatim. Leave EMPTY when using
# MULLVAD_EXIT above. This path skips the multihop enforcement -- you supplied
# the endpoint, so you own it -- and only warns if it is single-hop.
MULLVAD_WG_CONFIG=""
